Date: Mon, 19 Jul 93 23:14:46 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: Wage Competition

From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>

> Basic economics suggests that in a reasonably competitive labor
> market, folks should earn a wage about equal to their marginal product
> (how much more wealth is produced if they work than if they don't).
> So the first cut of an answer is to say that as long as you can keep
> just as productive, you should earn the same real wage.  If robots or
> Mexicans or whatever get even more productive, they earn more, but
> unless the issue is envy, you needn't worry.

How does this apply to the situation where others are able to produce
just as much as you, but are willing to do so for less than what would
be a living wage for you?  It would seem that although you can still produce
just as much as you used to, the market value of that production has dropped
due to your more-efficient competitors.  So you can be just as productive in
goods, but if the goods you produce drop in price, then you are less productive
in dollars.  For this reason, it seems that if your competitors become more
productive, you do need to worry for other reasons than envy.

Hal Finney
hfinney@shell.portal.com

