Date: Tue, 20 Jul 93 01:08:56 -0700
From: zz@garnet.berkeley.edu (Zachary Zaidman)
Subject: personal cancer experiences...

Hello everybody! I am back from a relatively long absence... 

I was away first because I was too busy and then because I got 
sick. I would like to share some of my experiences with this 
illness with you all. For those of you who are interested, 
following is a very brief overvew of what I have been going 
through:

A few months ago I noticed a tiny sharp bump on one of my 
testicles. It was dismissed three separate times by three 
different doctors in the university health center who said there 
was nothing to worry about. Eventually, I arranged to see a 
private urologist who immediately determined that we should 
do an ultrasound. That same evening I had checked in to the 
hospital for exploratory surgery which turned out to be a radical 
orchiectomy leaving me with one lonely 'odd ball' ;-), and a 
diagnosis of embryonal cell testicular cancer which I have since 
learned is the most common cancer in young men and the most 
curable form of solid cancer. After a month long crash course on 
testicular cancer, I am quite reassured that it is very unlikely 
that I will die from this disease. But I have already undergone a 
second and much more serious surgery to remove twenty six 
retroperitoneal (abdominal) lymph nodes, I am still stuck in San 
Diego recovering, and I have a little more "work" to do before 
being completely officially cured. Three lymph nodes already 
had microscopic cancer. Based on the most current research, 
this means that 80% of those in my condition will be 
permanently cured with the surgeries alone while about 20% 
will relapse in the lungs within about two years--a relapse 
with a 99% cure rate.

Here is the current challenge: I have been offered a choice to 
undergo two courses of "profilactic" or "adjuvant" 
chemotherapyy in order to virtaully guarantee no future 
relapses, OR to do nothing except watch and wait for two years 
getting monthly x rays and blood tests (tumor markers) and 
quarterly CAT scans. If I should replase, three or four courses of 
the same chemotherapy (blyomicine, vp16, and sisplatinum.) 
should virtually always be totally curative. 

All the data available indeed suggest that patients who wait 
have the same final outcome as those who receive adjuvant 
chemotherapy. There are plenty of reasons to decide either way 
but none of them are medical. This is very frustrating for me 
and I am still searching through the literature and talking to all 
kinds of experts trying to find answers to my concerns. On the 
one hand chemotherapy is very toxic--it causes infertility in 
about 50% of patients and, in very rare cases, it could result in 
more serious side effects such as secondary cancers, lung 
fibrosis, and more. On the other hand, cancer is no fun either. I 
don't want to go through chemotherapy if 80% of the time I 
would be doing it for nothing. In any case, experts are clear
only about the fact that there is no right or wrong choice. I 
have about three days to decide and I presently have no idea
what I will do. I have MUCH more to say about this 
predicament, the decision I am facing, and my experiences 
throughout this past month but I will reserve it for those of 
you who are interested in hearing more. I am personally very 
interested in your thoughts, questions, or opinions and I am 
happy to be back online as I have been missing this community.

Please respond either directly to zz@garnet.berkeley.edu or to 
the list with a copy to my personal account--for now...

I have been feeling very well lately and I look forward to 
continue living my long life with the perspectives I have gained 
throughout this ordeal. 

Best regards!

Zak
PS Any good extropian names for a one testicle mutant transhuman? :-)

More soon.

