Subject: Wage Competition

Hal Finney:
> How does this apply to the situation where others are able to produce
> just as much as you, but are willing to do so for less than what would
> be a living wage for you?

What is a real-world example of this?  The only examples I can
think of are (a) the formerly high-wage worker
has to reevaluate what he considers a "necessity" for life  -- 
usually things his parents, or his lower-wage competitors, 
consider[ed] luxuries, or (b) situations where the high-wage
worker has foolishly saddled himself with long-range debt
without wage or unemployment insurance.  If there is a big
cost of living difference, the formerly high-wage worker can move 
into the low-wage worker's neighborhood, and vice versa.  
If future wage shifts can be predicted with a wage futures
market, the long-term indebtedness, cost of living, and very 
expectations of the worker as to what is a "necessity" could be 
adjusted accordingly, and retraining costs better allocated.
(I'm not sure how well such a market would predict wages,
though).

